1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,660
Inside almost every cell of your
body is a set

2
00:00:03,670 --> 00:00:04,490
of instructions,

3
00:00:05,270 --> 00:00:06,730
three billion letters long.

4
00:00:07,470 --> 00:00:08,270
Your DNA.

5
00:00:08,583 --> 00:00:11,493
A single wrong letter in that
code can cause a

6
00:00:11,503 --> 00:00:12,643
devastating disease.

7
00:00:13,750 --> 00:00:16,770
For most of history, there was
nothing we could do about it.

8
00:00:17,070 --> 00:00:20,560
Then, scientists found a tool
that can find any spot

9
00:00:20,570 --> 00:00:23,610
in the genome, and cut it, with
incredible precision.

10
00:00:24,583 --> 00:00:25,783
It's called CRISPR.

11
00:00:26,223 --> 00:00:27,583
And it was borrowed from
bacteria.

12
00:00:29,567 --> 00:00:31,647
DNA is a long, twisted ladder.

13
00:00:32,247 --> 00:00:34,467
Each rung is a pair of chemical
letters:

14
00:00:34,907 --> 00:00:36,847
A, T, G and C.

15
00:00:37,407 --> 00:00:38,847
Their order spells out genes,

16
00:00:39,667 --> 00:00:41,387
the recipes for making proteins.

17
00:00:42,667 --> 00:00:45,487
If you printed out the DNA in
one human cell,

18
00:00:46,047 --> 00:00:48,487
it would fill around two hundred
thick phone books.

19
00:00:49,127 --> 00:00:52,527
And if you stretched it out, it
would be about two metres long.

20
00:00:53,333 --> 00:00:55,413
Just reading it was a colossal
task.

21
00:00:55,993 --> 00:00:59,283
The Human Genome Project took
thirteen years and about

22
00:00:59,293 --> 00:01:02,629
three billion dollars, finishing
in two thousand

23
00:01:02,639 --> 00:01:03,439
and three.

24
00:01:03,917 --> 00:01:04,717
Some diseases,

25
00:01:05,337 --> 00:01:08,307
like sickle cell disease, come
from a single

26
00:01:08,317 --> 00:01:10,537
misspelled letter in a single
gene.

27
00:01:11,333 --> 00:01:12,333
In sickle cell disease,

28
00:01:13,013 --> 00:01:15,893
red blood cells bend into stiff
crescent shapes.

29
00:01:16,353 --> 00:01:19,283
They get stuck in blood vessels,
causing terrible

30
00:01:19,293 --> 00:01:21,193
pain and organ damage.

31
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:24,460
It affects millions of people
around the world,

32
00:01:24,920 --> 00:01:25,880
many with African,

33
00:01:26,520 --> 00:01:28,840
Middle Eastern or South Asian
ancestry.

34
00:01:29,580 --> 00:01:32,730
Strangely, carrying one copy of
the gene helps

35
00:01:32,740 --> 00:01:35,810
protect against malaria, which
is why it became

36
00:01:35,820 --> 00:01:36,660
so common.

37
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:40,750
For decades, scientists dreamed
of fixing

38
00:01:40,760 --> 00:01:42,540
faulty genes directly.

39
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:45,860
But the early tools for editing
DNA were slow,

40
00:01:46,160 --> 00:01:46,960
expensive,

41
00:01:47,320 --> 00:01:48,120
and hard to aim.

42
00:01:48,917 --> 00:01:52,307
Tools called zinc fingers and
TALENs could be built

43
00:01:52,317 --> 00:01:53,557
to cut chosen spots,

44
00:01:54,177 --> 00:01:57,527
but designing each one could
take months of specialist

45
00:01:57,537 --> 00:01:58,337
work.

46
00:01:59,650 --> 00:02:01,770
The answer came from somewhere
unexpected.

47
00:02:02,410 --> 00:02:05,700
In the nineteen nineties, a
Spanish microbiologist

48
00:02:05,710 --> 00:02:08,820
named Francisco Mojica was
studying microbes

49
00:02:08,830 --> 00:02:10,130
from salt marshes.

50
00:02:10,917 --> 00:02:11,717
In their DNA,

51
00:02:12,297 --> 00:02:15,567
he noticed strange repeated
sequences, separated

52
00:02:15,577 --> 00:02:17,157
by unique spacers.

53
00:02:17,637 --> 00:02:21,047
A Japanese team led by Yoshizumi
Ishino had

54
00:02:21,057 --> 00:02:24,490
first spotted similar repeats in
nineteen eighty seven.

55
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:27,990
He and colleagues named them
CRISPR: clustered

56
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:31,360
regularly interspaced short
palindromic repeats.

57
00:02:32,333 --> 00:02:35,723
Around two thousand and five,
Mojica worked

58
00:02:35,733 --> 00:02:39,123
out what the spacers were:
snippets of DNA from

59
00:02:39,133 --> 00:02:41,993
viruses that had attacked the
bacteria before.

60
00:02:42,917 --> 00:02:46,387
Bacteria are in a never-ending
war with viruses

61
00:02:46,397 --> 00:02:47,477
called phages.

62
00:02:47,977 --> 00:02:51,027
Phages are thought to be the
most numerous biological

63
00:02:51,037 --> 00:02:54,207
entities on Earth, outnumbering
bacteria

64
00:02:54,217 --> 00:02:56,077
by around ten to one.

65
00:02:56,833 --> 00:02:58,373
CRISPR was an immune system.

66
00:02:58,833 --> 00:03:02,153
The bacteria kept a library of
mugshots of their enemies.

67
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:04,900
If the same virus attacked
again,

68
00:03:05,520 --> 00:03:08,950
the bacteria copied the matching
mugshot into a short

69
00:03:08,960 --> 00:03:10,200
strand of RNA.

70
00:03:11,140 --> 00:03:14,250
That strand guided a cutting
protein to the virus's

71
00:03:14,260 --> 00:03:14,710
DNA,

72
00:03:14,720 --> 00:03:15,920
and chopped it up.

73
00:03:16,417 --> 00:03:19,407
In two thousand and seven,
Rodolphe

74
00:03:19,417 --> 00:03:19,987
Barrangou,

75
00:03:19,997 --> 00:03:23,127
Philippe Horvath and colleagues
at the yoghurt company

76
00:03:23,137 --> 00:03:26,367
Danisco confirmed it, while
trying to protect

77
00:03:26,377 --> 00:03:29,547
the bacteria they used to make
yoghurt and cheese

78
00:03:29,557 --> 00:03:30,417
from viruses.

79
00:03:32,233 --> 00:03:35,573
The most important cutting
protein is called Cas nine.

80
00:03:36,417 --> 00:03:39,587
It comes from Streptococcus
pyogenes, the

81
00:03:39,597 --> 00:03:41,737
bacterium that causes strep
throat.

82
00:03:42,583 --> 00:03:46,073
In two thousand and eleven, the
French microbiologist

83
00:03:46,083 --> 00:03:48,953
Emmanuelle Charpentier met the
American

84
00:03:48,963 --> 00:03:52,333
biochemist Jennifer Doudna at a
conference in Puerto

85
00:03:52,343 --> 00:03:53,143
Rico.

86
00:03:53,303 --> 00:03:54,723
They decided to work together.

87
00:03:55,583 --> 00:03:57,803
Charpentier was working in
Sweden,

88
00:03:58,483 --> 00:04:00,023
Doudna in California.

89
00:04:00,683 --> 00:04:03,173
They collaborated across nine
time zones,

90
00:04:03,183 --> 00:04:05,483
by email and video calls.

91
00:04:06,417 --> 00:04:09,737
In June twenty twelve, they
published a landmark paper.

92
00:04:10,157 --> 00:04:13,327
They showed that Cas nine could
be reprogrammed with a guide

93
00:04:13,337 --> 00:04:16,257
RNA to cut any DNA sequence they
chose.

94
00:04:17,167 --> 00:04:18,407
Here's how it works.

95
00:04:18,927 --> 00:04:21,087
Scientists write a short guide
sequence,

96
00:04:21,487 --> 00:04:22,827
about twenty letters long,

97
00:04:23,427 --> 00:04:24,427
that matches the target.

98
00:04:25,250 --> 00:04:27,410
Cas nine slides along the DNA.

99
00:04:27,970 --> 00:04:29,980
When the guide finds its perfect
match,

100
00:04:29,990 --> 00:04:33,000
Cas nine cuts both strands of
the double

101
00:04:33,010 --> 00:04:33,810
helix.

102
00:04:34,500 --> 00:04:36,620
The cell then rushes to repair
the break.

103
00:04:37,660 --> 00:04:40,570
Scientists can use that moment
to switch a gene off,

104
00:04:40,580 --> 00:04:44,030
or to slip in a new, corrected
piece of

105
00:04:44,040 --> 00:04:44,840
DNA.

106
00:04:45,417 --> 00:04:47,337
It's like a word processor for
genes:

107
00:04:47,997 --> 00:04:48,787
find,

108
00:04:48,797 --> 00:04:49,777
cut, and replace.

109
00:04:50,750 --> 00:04:53,920
Within months, teams led by Feng
Zhang and George

110
00:04:53,930 --> 00:04:57,050
Church showed that it worked in
human cells too.

111
00:04:57,833 --> 00:05:00,293
CRISPR was cheap, fast and easy.

112
00:05:00,693 --> 00:05:04,023
Labs all over the world began
using it almost

113
00:05:04,033 --> 00:05:04,833
overnight.

114
00:05:05,500 --> 00:05:08,290
The discovery also sparked a
long legal battle,

115
00:05:08,300 --> 00:05:11,190
between the University of
California, where

116
00:05:11,200 --> 00:05:14,070
Doudna works, and the Broad
Institute in

117
00:05:14,080 --> 00:05:14,770
Massachusetts,

118
00:05:14,780 --> 00:05:16,940
over who owns the patents.

119
00:05:17,833 --> 00:05:21,203
In twenty twenty, Charpentier
and Doudna

120
00:05:21,213 --> 00:05:23,373
won the Nobel Prize in
Chemistry.

121
00:05:24,073 --> 00:05:27,503
It was the first time a science
Nobel had been shared only by

122
00:05:27,513 --> 00:05:28,313
women.

123
00:05:29,483 --> 00:05:32,883
The first big medical success
came for sickle cell disease.

124
00:05:33,833 --> 00:05:37,003
Doctors take a patient's own
blood stem cells,

125
00:05:37,013 --> 00:05:40,503
and use CRISPR to switch on a
gene for a type of

126
00:05:40,513 --> 00:05:43,453
haemoglobin we all make before
we're born.

127
00:05:44,333 --> 00:05:47,743
Those edited cells are put back,
and begin making healthy

128
00:05:47,753 --> 00:05:48,813
red blood cells.

129
00:05:49,750 --> 00:05:53,220
Victoria Gray, from Mississippi,
was the first American

130
00:05:53,230 --> 00:05:56,020
with sickle cell disease to be
treated this way,

131
00:05:56,030 --> 00:05:57,920
in twenty nineteen.

132
00:05:57,930 --> 00:05:59,250
Her pain crises stopped.

133
00:06:00,167 --> 00:06:03,257
In late twenty twenty three,
regulators in Britain

134
00:06:03,267 --> 00:06:05,837
and the United States approved
the treatment,

135
00:06:05,847 --> 00:06:07,997
called Casgevy.

136
00:06:08,007 --> 00:06:10,487
It was the world's first
approved CRISPR medicine.

137
00:06:11,417 --> 00:06:14,627
Scientists are now testing
CRISPR against inherited

138
00:06:14,637 --> 00:06:15,437
blindness,

139
00:06:15,757 --> 00:06:16,557
high cholesterol,

140
00:06:17,177 --> 00:06:19,697
some cancers, and other genetic
diseases.

141
00:06:20,667 --> 00:06:22,847
Newer versions are even more
precise.

142
00:06:23,707 --> 00:06:27,177
Base editing, invented in David
Liu's lab in

143
00:06:27,187 --> 00:06:30,457
twenty sixteen, can change a
single letter without

144
00:06:30,467 --> 00:06:31,807
cutting both strands.

145
00:06:32,787 --> 00:06:35,367
Prime editing can search and
replace short passages.

146
00:06:36,417 --> 00:06:39,367
CRISPR has even been turned into
fast diagnostic

147
00:06:39,377 --> 00:06:40,347
tests,

148
00:06:40,357 --> 00:06:43,677
which can detect the genetic
material of viruses,

149
00:06:44,257 --> 00:06:46,597
like the one that causes COVID
nineteen.

150
00:06:47,500 --> 00:06:50,930
In twenty twenty five, doctors
in Philadelphia treated

151
00:06:50,940 --> 00:06:54,230
a baby boy named KJ, born with a
rare

152
00:06:54,240 --> 00:06:57,470
and deadly liver disorder, with
a gene-editing

153
00:06:57,480 --> 00:06:59,360
therapy designed just for him.

154
00:07:00,900 --> 00:07:03,080
CRISPR is also changing farming.

155
00:07:03,680 --> 00:07:06,920
Researchers are using it to make
crops that resist disease,

156
00:07:07,380 --> 00:07:10,320
survive drought, or simply keep
fresh for longer.

157
00:07:11,167 --> 00:07:14,377
Some scientists want to use it
to spread genes through

158
00:07:14,387 --> 00:07:17,307
wild mosquitoes that stop them
carrying malaria,

159
00:07:18,067 --> 00:07:21,417
a disease that kills hundreds of
thousands of people every

160
00:07:21,427 --> 00:07:22,227
year.

161
00:07:22,417 --> 00:07:25,347
In twenty twenty four, surgeons
in Boston

162
00:07:25,357 --> 00:07:28,387
transplanted a kidney from a
gene-edited pig

163
00:07:28,397 --> 00:07:31,767
into a living patient, part of a
push to solve

164
00:07:31,777 --> 00:07:34,057
the shortage of organs for
transplant.

165
00:07:35,983 --> 00:07:38,383
But CRISPR also raises hard
questions.

166
00:07:39,417 --> 00:07:42,777
Editing a patient's blood cells
affects only that person.

167
00:07:43,237 --> 00:07:45,897
But editing an embryo would
change every cell,

168
00:07:46,317 --> 00:07:49,687
including eggs and sperm, so the
changes would pass

169
00:07:49,697 --> 00:07:50,717
to future generations.

170
00:07:51,917 --> 00:07:54,537
In twenty eighteen, a Chinese
scientist,

171
00:07:54,997 --> 00:07:58,367
He Jiankui, announced that he
had secretly edited the DNA

172
00:07:58,377 --> 00:08:01,477
of human embryos, and that twin
girls had been born.

173
00:08:02,333 --> 00:08:05,383
Scientists around the world
condemned it as reckless

174
00:08:05,393 --> 00:08:06,393
and unethical.

175
00:08:07,013 --> 00:08:09,133
He was sentenced to three years
in prison.

176
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:12,980
The twins were known by the
names Lulu and Nana.

177
00:08:13,540 --> 00:08:16,910
He was released in twenty twenty
two, and the long-term effects

178
00:08:16,920 --> 00:08:19,240
of his edits on the girls are
still unknown.

179
00:08:20,167 --> 00:08:23,097
Most countries now ban or
tightly restrict

180
00:08:23,107 --> 00:08:24,967
editing embryos for pregnancy.

181
00:08:25,607 --> 00:08:29,057
Deciding where the limits should
be is a question for all of

182
00:08:29,067 --> 00:08:29,737
us,

183
00:08:29,747 --> 00:08:30,867
not just scientists.

184
00:08:31,917 --> 00:08:32,937
There are other worries,

185
00:08:33,677 --> 00:08:34,427
too.

186
00:08:34,437 --> 00:08:36,717
CRISPR can sometimes cut the
wrong place.

187
00:08:37,297 --> 00:08:39,947
And the first approved treatment
costs over two million dollars

188
00:08:39,957 --> 00:08:42,437
per patient in the United
States,

189
00:08:43,057 --> 00:08:43,857
so who can afford it?

190
00:08:45,317 --> 00:08:48,787
A defence system that bacteria
evolved to fight viruses

191
00:08:48,797 --> 00:08:52,047
has become one of the most
powerful tools in the

192
00:08:52,057 --> 00:08:53,097
history of medicine.

193
00:08:54,000 --> 00:08:55,860
We can now rewrite the code of
life.

194
00:08:56,320 --> 00:08:58,660
The question is no longer
whether we can.

195
00:08:59,140 --> 00:09:01,160
It's how wisely we choose to.
